{"kind":"request","text":"codex: the git-identity repair closed today's instance; this is the class it leaves open. Not urgent, and explicitly not a criticism of ce6e64e2, which I approved and want landed first.\n\nWHAT I FOUND WHILE REVIEWING THAT HEAD. I probed whether the repair actually removed the suite's dependence on ambient git configuration or only the identity part of it. Two settings, both against the repaired head, both with GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null:\n\ninit.defaultBranch=trunk is harmless. cmd/gitseq-mcp passes, ok 7.331s.\n\ncommit.gpgsign=true breaks all three TestReviewTool cases. Passing -c user.name and -c user.email does not stop git attempting to sign, so the fixture commit fails on any machine where commit signing is on globally.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A DEFECT IN CODEX'S HEAD, and I checked this before writing the request rather than assuming it. I ran the established call sites under the same setting: cmd/gs TestSuccessionAdmissionPreflightsEveryActWithoutAppending and TestSuccessionAdmissionAppliesResidentRequestCapWithoutAppending fail identically. So the inline convention itself is gpgsign-fragile, repository-wide, and was so long before this week. Matching the convention was the right call in an emergency head. The convention is what has the gap.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE, plainly. Any contributor who has commit signing enabled globally — which is ordinary practice, and is what someone security-minded is most likely to have turned on — cannot run this test suite, and gets a wall of failures that look like real regressions. We would not learn about it from CI, because a GitHub runner has signing off. It is exactly the same shape as the defect we just fixed, with the polarity reversed: that one passed on every laptop and failed on the runner, this one passes on the runner and fails on some laptops.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. I am not asking you to adopt the shared helper I floated in the previous request. You judged against it, I looked for evidence against your judgement and found evidence for it, and I recorded that in the verdict. Whatever closes this should be chosen on its own merits: a commit.gpgsign=false alongside the identity at each site, an environment scrub inside the test process, a helper, or something else. There are sixteen sites now, so a per-site fix is a real if boring cost, and that argument may land differently than it did for one line.\n\nMeasure before choosing. I probed two settings; there may be others — core.autocrlf, core.hooksPath, commit.template and gpg.format are the ones I would look at next. Say what you actually tested rather than what you expect, and if some setting cannot be defended against, name it rather than implying full coverage.","body":{"conditions":"The Go test suite passes on a machine whose ambient git configuration has commit signing enabled, and the choice of mechanism is yours rather than dictated here. Reproduce the failure first and state it: with GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null and a GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL containing commit.gpgsign=true, show cmd/gitseq-mcp TestReviewTool and cmd/gs TestSuccessionAdmissionPreflightsEveryActWithoutAppending failing before the change and passing after. Survey the ambient git settings a real contributor plausibly sets — at least core.autocrlf, core.hooksPath, commit.template and gpg.format alongside commit.gpgsign and init.defaultBranch — say which you tested, which are defended against afterwards, and name any you decided not to defend and why. Cover every git-invoking test site rather than the two named here; there are sixteen inline-identity sites today and a fix that reaches only the ones I happened to probe is worse than none, because it reads as coverage. Prove mutation-sensitivity by removing the new defence at one site and watching a named assertion fail, then reverting. Record the judgement on mechanism — per-site configuration, a shared helper, or a process-level environment scrub — with the reason, including the cost argument across sixteen sites, since that argument may land differently than it did for a single line. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review. Do not merge. Sequenced after the git-identity repair at ce6e64e2 lands, after the identifier consolidation at 500bc9b1, and after the chess lanes; nothing here is urgent.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}